/* book Worked example 2: Q: For a long time a 12 V source has driven R₁ = 4 Ω, R₂ = 2 Ω and L = 0.6 H in series. At t = 0 a switch shorts R₂. Find i(t) and vL(t) for t ≥ 0, name the forced and natural parts, and find when the current reaches 2.9 A.
   1. Before the switch the inductor is a short: i(0⁻) = 12/(4 + 2) = 2 A, and continuity gives i(0⁺) = 2 A.
   2. After the switch only R₁ remains: i(∞) = 12/4 = 3 A and τ = L/R₁ = 0.6/4 = 0.15 s, so 1/τ = 6.67 s⁻¹.
   3. i(t) = 3 + (2 − 3) e−t/0.15 = 3 − e−6.67t A. Forced part 3 A; natural part −e−6.67t A.
   4. vL = L di/dt = 0.6 × 6.67 × e−6.67t = 4 e−6.67t V. Check at 0⁺: KVL gives 12 − 4 × 2 = 4 V. ✓
   5. Set 3 − e−t/0.15 = 2.9: e−t/0.15 = 0.1, so t = 0.15 ln 10 = 0.345 s.
   Answer: i(t) = 3 − e−6.67t A (forced 3 A, natural −e−6.67t A), vL(t) = 4e−6.67t V, and i = 2.9 A at t = 0.345 s. */
